<urn:atm:triage:finding/AI3152-WINTCP-CAP-004>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AI3152-WINTCP-CAP-004" ;
  triage:title "Windows local TCP exceeds documented accept cap and silently drops overload connections" ;
  triage:description "Reported by arch-ctm's phase-end critical web/transport review against integrate/phase-ai-31-33@577067ae. local_tcp_transport.rs:75 sets MAX_CONCURRENT_CONNECTIONS=128 although requirements.md:3922 specifies 64; spawn_windows_connection at :435-437 returns Ok(()) when full, writing neither an HTTP typed error nor structured degradation. That is a direct no-response/client-reset path and violates requirements.md:3931-3932. required_correction: Enforce the documented cap and send a deterministic typed overload response, with a Windows saturation test." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-AI" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "correctness" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable true ;
  triage:sweepScope "file_only" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-08-02T01:23:08Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:AI-PHASE-END-REVIEW ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-08-02T01:23:08Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:progressNote "AI3152-PHASEEND-QA-1 (commit d2891ab5): arch-qa and ruthless-boundary-qa PASSed this finding on a non-Windows host, citing MAX_CONCURRENT_CONNECTIONS now set to 64 (local_tcp_transport.rs:77) and spawn_windows_connection calling reject_overloaded_windows_connection (~469-489) with a typed DaemonUnavailable overload response instead of silent Ok(()). req-qa flagged the Windows path as unverified on a non-Windows host (ATM-QA-202). quality-mgr independently confirmed live Windows CI on PR #683 (job 91442864724, run 30727862862): Test (windows-latest) FAILS with a genuine compile error, not a test-assertion failure -- error[E0599]: no method named `try_register` found for struct `ActiveConnectionRegistry`. Root cause: the new test windows_connection_cap_rejects_the_sixty_fifth_client_with_a_typed_response (local_tcp_transport.rs:1399) does `let registry = ActiveConnectionRegistry::default();` (a bare struct), but try_register is `pub(crate) fn try_register(self: &Arc<Self>, ...)` (active_connection_registry.rs:70) -- an arbitrary-self-type receiver only callable on Arc<ActiveConnectionRegistry>. Every other call site in the crate correctly wraps in Arc::new(...); this one test does not. This elevates req-qa's caution from unverified to confirmed-broken: the Windows build does not compile. Remains open; requires wrapping the test's registry in Arc::new(ActiveConnectionRegistry::default()) and a real Windows CI green run before this can close." ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AI3152-WINTCP-CAP-004/cap-constant> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AI3152-WINTCP-CAP-004/silent-drop> ;
  triage:triageRecordVersion "3" ;
  triage:closedAt "2026-08-02T21:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:closedBy "quality-mgr" ;
  triage:progressNote "Fixed. arch-ctm pushed cb46830b directly to integrate/phase-ai-31-33: `let registry = ActiveConnectionRegistry::default();` at local_tcp_transport.rs:1400 (the Windows-only capacity test) is now `Arc::new(ActiveConnectionRegistry::default())`, matching every other call site's Arc<Self> receiver contract for try_register. Confirmed the fix commit is an ancestor of current HEAD (cb650166) via git log/branch --contains. Confirmed live Windows CI green on run 30766041592 (head b8bb6df2, which already contains cb46830b): both 'Just lint (windows-latest)' and 'Test (windows-latest)' jobs report conclusion=success -- the E0599 compile error from AI3152-PHASEEND-QA-1 is gone. Independently re-verified the underlying correctness fix is still in place at current HEAD: MAX_CONCURRENT_CONNECTIONS=64 (local_tcp_transport.rs:77) and spawn_windows_connection calls reject_overloaded_windows_connection (local_tcp_transport.rs:438/470) on overload instead of silently returning Ok(()). All three original concerns (cap value, typed overload response, Windows compile) are resolved." .

